FOR HIDDE — this bot's GitHub App token cannot push changes to files under
.github/workflows/ (rejected: "refusing to allow a GitHub App to create or
update workflow `.github/workflows/ios.yml` without `workflows` permission").
So this fix could not be committed directly; it lives here as a patch instead.

To apply: `git apply PENDING_HIDDE_ios-ci-grep-fix.patch` from the repo root,
then review, commit and push it yourself, then delete this file (or ask a
run to delete it once applied). Also worth granting the GitHub App the
`workflows` permission if you want future runs to land this kind of fix
without a manual step.

WHAT IT FIXES: the iOS app CI's `test` job, "Build and test" step, gated on
a blanket `grep -q "error:"` across the whole xcodebuild log, including the
app's own console output. On 2026-09-01 (run 33547321736) the simulator's
boringssl SSL retry logging ("error:1000007d") tripped that grep two lines
after xcodebuild itself printed "** TEST SUCCEEDED **", failing a healthy
run. This narrows the check to XCTest's actual failure line format
(file:line: error: -[Class test]), which the existing COMPILE_ERR pattern
in the same step already distinguishes from a compiler error by column
presence, so the new pattern follows an established convention rather than
inventing one.

--- a/.github/workflows/ios.yml
+++ b/.github/workflows/ios.yml
@@ -247,9 +247,19 @@ jobs:
             echo "::error::The app did not compile, so no test could run. The compiler errors are above."
             exit 1
           fi
-          if grep -q "error:" /tmp/xcodebuild.log; then
+          # A real XCTest failure line, not a blanket "error:" match. The
+          # simulator's own console output can carry that substring on a
+          # healthy run, and did on 2026-09-01: boringssl logs
+          # "error:1000007d" mid SSL-handshake retry and the app goes on to
+          # work fine, but the blanket grep below read it as a failed test
+          # and exited 1 two lines after xcodebuild itself had printed
+          # "** TEST SUCCEEDED **". XCTest's own failure line is always
+          # "<file>:<line>: error: -[Class test] : ...", which the
+          # COMPILE_ERR pattern above never matches because that one always
+          # carries a column number.
+          if grep -qE ': error: -\[' /tmp/xcodebuild.log; then
             echo "::error::The app built. Tests FAILED, named below. This is not a build problem."
-            grep -E ": error: -\[" /tmp/xcodebuild.log | sed 's/^.*: error: //' | sort -u
+            grep -E ': error: -\[' /tmp/xcodebuild.log | sed 's/^.*: error: //' | sort -u
             exit 1
           fi
           # grep swallows the exit code, so ask xcodebuild again for the verdict.
